  keywords="Eurocert Asia,Eurocert Asia,ISO certification,CE marking,food safety certification,FSSC 22000,BRCGS,GFSI,social audits,Sedex,SMETA,SA8000,inspection services,certification body,India,management systems,ISO 9001,ISO 14001,ISO 45001"
>
<style is:global>@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Free";font-style:normal;font-weight:900;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-solid-900.woff2) format('woff2')}@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Brands";font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-brands-400.woff2) format('woff2')}@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Free";font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-regular-400.woff2) format('woff2')}</style>
<section class="relative min-h-[55vh] flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden bg-[#030a16]"><div class="absolute inset-0 z-0"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_webp,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782368189/eurocert/m12.png.webp" class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-30" alt="ISO 39001 Road Traffic Safety"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-[#030a16]/70 via-[#030a16]/40 to-[#030a16]"></div></div><div class="container mx-auto px-6 relative z-10 text-center py-20"><div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-eurogold-400/30 bg-eurogold-400/5 mb-6"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-eurogold-400 animate-pulse"></span><span class="heading-font text-[10px] text-eurogold-400 tracking-[0.3em] uppercase font-bold">Road traffic safety management</span></div><h1 class="heading-font text-4xl sm:text-6xl md:text-7xl font-bold text-white mb-3 tracking-tight" style="text-shadow:0 0 30px rgba(212,168,67,0.3)">ISO 39001  -  Road Traffic Safety</h1><p class="text-white/90 text-sm md:text-base max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">Framework for reducing deaths and serious injuries related to road traffic crashes.</p></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="grid lg:grid-cols-5 gap-10 items-start"><div class="lg:col-span-3 space-y-6"><div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Standard Overview</h4><h2 class="heading-font text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">ISO 39001  -  <span class="text-euro-600">Road Traffic Safety</span></h2></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">To assess applicability, define organizational boundaries, the standard edition, services or activities, locations and intended assurance route before setting an audit scope.</p><div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-euro-100 bg-euro-50/40"><div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3"><div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-euro-600 flex items-center justify-center"><i class="fas fa-info-circle text-white text-xs"></i></div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">What is ISO 39001?</h4></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">ISO 39001 specifies requirements for a road traffic safety (RTS) management system. It helps organisations influence road-safety outcomes by identifying relevant factors, setting objectives and managing activities that affect risks of death or serious injury. It is not a vehicle certification standard, driving licence or guarantee that incidents will not occur. The system should reflect the organisation's role in the road traffic system and the evidence it can influence.</p></div></div><div class="lg:col-span-2 relative rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-slate-100 h-[320px] md:h-[400px] group"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_webp,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782368189/eurocert/m12.png.webp" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-700 group-hover:scale-105" alt="ISO 39001 Road Traffic Safety"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-slate-900/20 via-transparent to-transparent"></div></div></div></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-[#f8fafc]"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="text-center mb-10"><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Discuss the standard, road-safety scope and applicable audit criteria for your organisation.</p><div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#030a16] bg-eurogold-400 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-eurogold-300 transition-all shadow-lg shadow-eurogold-400/20" href="/contact/">Get Started <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-[9px]"></i></a><a href="tel:+919316012883" onclick="return gtag_report_conversion('tel:+919316012883');" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-white border border-white/15 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-white/5 transition-all"><i class="fas fa-phone text-[9px]"></i> Call Us Now</a></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white" aria-labelledby="standard-details"><div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-5xl space-y-8"><h2 id="standard-details" class="heading-font text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Applying ISO 39001 road traffic safety management in practice</h2><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Who should consider it?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">ISO 39001 may be relevant to transport and logistics companies, public transport operators, fleet owners, road authorities, emergency services, organisations managing vehicle movements and businesses whose work creates road exposure. It can also help organisations influence safety through procurement, route planning, vehicle specifications or contractor requirements. Define operations, people, vehicles, routes, locations and contractors in scope. Do not ignore material outsourced risks if the organisation can influence them.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Key requirements</h3><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed"><li>Analyze organizational context, parties, legal and other obligations, and the organisation's role, impact and influence in the road traffic system.</li><li>Identify relevant safety performance factors, such as exposure, safe speeds, roads and roadsides, travel, vehicles and post-crash response, as applicable.</li><li>Set measurable RTS objectives and plans linked to evidence, responsibilities, resources and realistic improvement opportunities.</li><li>Manage competence, awareness, communication, operational planning, contractors, procurement and emergency preparedness where they affect road safety.</li><li>Monitor performance and incidents, evaluate compliance, investigate relevant events, audit and review the system, and implement corrective action and improvement.</li></ul></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Certification and audit lifecycle</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Stage 1 reviews the proposed RTS management-system scope, organizational context, performance factors, objectives, plans and readiness. Stage 2 evaluates implementation and effectiveness by sampling operational controls, fleet or route arrangements, contractor management, competence, incident data, monitoring and staff awareness. Findings must be handled under the applicable scheme before an independent decision. Surveillance audits check continuing conformity at planned intervals; recertification is considered before the certificate cycle ends. An audit samples the system; it is not assurance that incidents will not happen. The audit plan depends on fleet size, operating patterns, sites, shifts, routes, outsourced transport and maturity. Timing is scope-dependent; no unsupported duration or result should be promised.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Documents to prepare</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Prepare RTS scope and context analysis; party and obligation registers; risk and performance-factor assessments; objectives, plans and indicators; fleet and journey records; vehicle inspection and maintenance evidence; driver competence and training; route and scheduling controls; contractor and procurement requirements; incident and near-miss data; emergency-response arrangements; monitoring and compliance reviews; internal audits; management review; and corrective actions. Protect personal information in driver and incident records.</p></div><div><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Indicators should fit the organization's influence and available evidence. A fleet operator might track exposure, vehicle condition, journey planning, driver competence, incidents and near misses, while a road authority may focus on hazards and corrective actions within its control. Use definitions that remain consistent over time, explain data limitations and review trends alongside operational changes. A single indicator cannot describe the whole safety system, so pair performance results with evidence about preventive controls and learning.</p><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Frequently asked questions</h3><div class="space-y-4"><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does ISO 39001 apply only to transport companies?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. It may suit any organisation whose activities influence road safety, including fleet operators, road authorities and businesses with vehicle movements.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does certification guarantee fewer accidents?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. The system supports systematic risk management and improvement but cannot guarantee that crashes or injuries will not occur.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">What is included in scope?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The organisation defines relevant operations, sites, vehicles, journeys, people and contractors; scope should reflect activities and road-safety influence.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">What performance factors might be considered?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Depending on context, factors can include exposure, speed, road conditions, travel modes, vehicle safety and post-crash response.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">How is certification maintained?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Where certification is granted, planned surveillance and recertification follow the applicable scheme and continued-conformity requirements.</p></article></div></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
